        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "51367f2a-6ae5-5975-8d46-e9ff3239d111",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Korematsu v. United States; Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire\n- People: Eric Yamamoto; Fred Korematsu\n- Organizations: United States Supreme Court\n- Concepts: Executive Order 9066; Japanese American internment; fighting words doctrine; First Amendment freedom of speech\n- Events: World War II\n\nFacts:\n- In 1942, Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire, 315 U.S. 568, was a United States Supreme Court case in which the Court articulated the fighting words doctrine as a limitation on First Amendment freedom of speech.\n- In 1944, Korematsu v. United States, 323 U.S. 214, was a landmark United States Supreme Court case about the constitutionality of Executive Order 9066.\n- During World War II, Executive Order 9066 ordered Japanese Americans into internment camps regardless of citizenship.\n- Flowing from the landmark Korematsu v. United States case, Eric Yamamoto is known for serving on Fred Korematsu's legal team.",
